# ── What the review established ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ MachineDeploymentSpec's ROLLOUT POLICY MOVED IN v1beta2 AND THE OLD PATH IS SILENTLY PRUNED.
#
#   v1beta1:  spec.strategy.rollingUpdate.{maxSurge,maxUnavailable}, spec.strategy.deletePolicy
#   v1beta2:  spec.rollout.strategy.rollingUpdate.{maxSurge,maxUnavailable}
#             spec.deletion.order, spec.rollout.after, spec.remediation.maxInFlight
#
# A chart copied from a v1beta1 template applies cleanly, is admitted, and drops the rollout policy on
# the floor. Both `bootstrap.configRef` and `infrastructureRef` also became version-less
# ContractVersionedObjectReferences.
#
# ⚠ NEITHER MachineDeploymentSpec NOR MachineSpec CARRIES A SINGLE `+kubebuilder:default`, AND YET FOUR
# THINGS ARE DEFAULTED — BY A MUTATING WEBHOOK. `core/webhooks/admission/machinedeployment.go` writes
# spec.replicas (honouring the autoscaler annotations, else 1), the whole rollout strategy (RollingUpdate,
# maxSurge 1, maxUnavailable 0), two `cluster.x-k8s.io/*` labels into BOTH the selector and the template
# labels, and a `v` prefix onto spec.template.spec.version. ⚠ THIS IS WHY `Matches` IS CONTAINMENT, and
# it is a DIFFERENT reason from the one three earlier providers give: the defaulting is a webhook rather
# than a CRD marker, so an equality comparison fails against a real API server and passes in BOTH
# conformance suites — a derived CRD stub has no webhook behind it any more than it has defaults.
#
# ⚠ THE WEBHOOK ALSO VALIDATES THAT spec.selector MATCHES spec.template.metadata.labels, so those two
# maps are built from one expression in C# and from one helper here. It further requires one of
# `spec.template.spec.bootstrap.dataSecretName` or `.configRef`.
#
# ⚠ THE KubevirtMachineTemplate SPEC IS SEVEN LEVELS DEEP AND EVERY LEVEL IS SOMEBODY ELSE'S TYPE:
#
#   spec.template.spec.virtualMachineTemplate.spec.template.spec.domain
#     │      │      │            │              │      │      └ kubevirtv1.VirtualMachineInstanceSpec
#     │      │      │            │              │      └ json:"template" (required)
#     │      │      │            │              └ kubevirtv1.VirtualMachineSpec
#     │      │      │            └ CAPK's OWN VirtualMachineTemplateSpec, not kubevirtv1's
#     │      │      └ KubevirtMachineSpec
#     │      └ KubevirtMachineTemplateResource
#     └ KubevirtMachineTemplateSpec
#
# Note also that the Go field BootstrapCheckSpec has the JSON key `virtualMachineBootstrapCheck`, and
# that its inner key is `checkStrategy` rather than `bootstrapCheckStrategy`.
#
# ⚠ CAPK APPENDS OBJECTS AND VOLUMES THIS CHART MUST NOT RENDER. Its machine controller creates
# `<bootstrapDataSecretName>-userdata` with an injected `capk` user and SSH key, appends a
# CloudInitConfigDrive volume and a matching virtio disk, force-overwrites the VM's metadata, and
# prefixes dataVolumeTemplates names with `<machine-name>-`. A template that supplied its own cloud-init
# volume would end up with two.
#
# ⚠ KubevirtMachineTemplateSpec IS IMMUTABLE. `pkg/webhookhandler/validator.go` is a validating-only
# webhook whose whole content is "KubevirtMachineTemplateSpec is immutable" on update. ⚠ THAT IS WHY
# /properties/size AND /properties/osDiskSize ARE `@immutable` IN THIS CHART: a PUT that changed either
# would be refused by the API server on the second pass, forever, with the resource stuck InProgress.
# Resizing a pool is replacing it, and Cluster API's own idiom for that is a NEW template name — which
# is what the `upgradeNodeImage` action's mechanism will be.
#
# ⚠ THE INSTANCETYPE IS CLUSTER-SCOPED AND THIS CHART DOES NOT CREATE IT.
# `VirtualMachineClusterInstancetype` is scope: Cluster (instancetype.kubevirt.io/v1beta1, plural
# virtualmachineclusterinstancetypes); `VirtualMachineInstancetype` is the namespaced one. An
# InstancetypeMatcher with an EMPTY kind resolves to the cluster-scoped type — `pkg/instancetype/find/
# spec.go` puts "" in the same switch case — so writing the kind out is correct today and is written
# out anyway, because a default that changes silently is worse than a line.
#
# ⚠ ONLY THREE `+kubebuilder:default` MARKERS EXIST IN CAPK's api/v1alpha1, and two of them are status
# fields: KubevirtClusterStatus.Ready=false, KubevirtMachineStatus.Ready=false, and
# VirtualMachineBootstrapCheckSpec.CheckStrategy=ssh (enum: none;ssh).
#
# ── The weak claims ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ NodeImageRepository AND ITS TAG WERE NOT READ OFF A REGISTRY. `quay.io/capk/ubuntu-2404-container-disk`
# tagged with ManagedClusters.PinnedPatch's value. A tag that does not exist is a VM that never boots,
# with the reason in a DataVolume's events — three objects below anything this platform reads.
# conformance.yaml § owed, `node-image-is-unverified`.
#
# ⚠ `virtualMachineBootstrapCheck` IS NOT RENDERED, SO THE CRD's `ssh` DEFAULT STANDS — which is what
# the control-plane provider's own published KubeVirt template does. It is nonetheless the riskiest
# default on this type: an SSH bootstrap check needs a key CAPK generates while reconciling the
# KubevirtCluster, and `cluster.x-k8s.io/managed-by: kamaji` makes it skip that object entirely.
# conformance.yaml § owed, `bootstrap-check-may-never-complete`.
upstream-webhook: cluster-api ships a MUTATING webhook for MachineDeployment that writes replicas, the rollout strategy, two labels and a version prefix; cluster-api-provider-kubevirt ships one validating webhook, for KubevirtMachineTemplate immutability, and no mutating webhook at all
